00:49:57.180 --> 00:50:11.190
<v Alex Blumberg>Act Three, "I'm the One who Knocks." Author Ben Loory has this short story about the 180 that awaits us all. And a quick note to our podcast listeners, there is a curse word that in the podcast we have not beeped. Here's Ben Loory.
<v Alex Blumberg>第三部分，“我是那个敲门的人”。作者本·卢里有一个关于等待着我们所有人的180度大转弯的短篇故事。给我们的播客听众提个醒，在播客中我们没有对一个脏话进行消音。这是本·卢里。

00:50:11.190 --> 00:50:27.800
<v Ben Loory>A man was walking down a long, long road when he saw a figure approaching from the distance. The man kept going, wondering who it was, when suddenly he realized it was Death. "Shit," said the man.
<v Ben Loory>一个男人正走在一条漫长的路上，这时他看到远处有一个身影走过来。男人继续走，好奇那人是谁，当突然意识到那是死神时。“靠，”男人说。

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:41.480
<v Ben Loory>He turned and looked around. There was a tree nearby. One with lots of leaves. So the man ran to it and climbed up in the branches and hid amongst the fruits there, peering out.
<v Ben Loory>他转过身环顾四周。附近有一棵树。树上有很多叶子。所以男人跑向它，爬上枝头，躲在果实中间，向外张望。

00:50:41.480 --> 00:50:59.370
<v Ben Loory>After a while, he saw Death coming down the road. He held very still and tried not to breathe. Keep going, he thought. Keep going, keep going. But Death didn't obey. Death stopped.
<v Ben Loory>过了一会儿，他看到死神沿着路走来。他一动不动，努力不呼吸。继续走，他想。继续走，继续走。但死神没有遵从。死神停了下来。

00:50:59.370 --> 00:51:20.720
<v Ben Loory>The man felt his heart constrict as death turned to the tree. He felt the blood freeze in his veins as Death looked up. Somehow he knew that despite all the leaves, Death could see him there in his hiding place. How, thought the man. How, if he can't see me, how does he know that I'm here?
<v Ben Loory>当死神转向那棵树时，男人感到心脏收缩。当死神抬头望去时，他感到血液在血管里冻结。不知怎的，他知道尽管叶子茂密，死神还是能看到他在那里的藏身之处。怎么会，男人想。怎么会，如果他看不见我，他怎么知道我在这里？

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:35.970
<v Ben Loory>And then the men realized that in the grip of fear, his hands were shaking, and they were shaking the tree. Oh, god, thought the man. I have to make them stop. He gripped the branches as tightly as he could.
<v Ben Loory>然后男人意识到在恐惧的驱使下，他的手在颤抖，它们在摇晃那棵树。噢，天哪，男人想。我必须让它们停下来。他尽可能紧地抓住树枝。

00:51:35.970 --> 00:51:49.940
<v Ben Loory>But the more he tried to hold them still, the more they shook and shook. And the man began to despair. And in the road, Death smiled.
<v Ben Loory>但他越努力让它们保持静止，它们就摇晃得越厉害。男人开始绝望。而在路上，死神笑了。

00:51:49.940 --> 00:52:05.110
<v Ben Loory>And right then it happened, the worst possible thing. One of the fruits detached from the tree. The man shaking had loosened it and loosened it and loosened it. And now it broke free and it fell.
<v Ben Loory>就在那时发生了，最糟糕的事情。其中一个果实脱离了树。男人的摇晃不断地松动它、松动它。现在它挣脱了，掉了下去。

00:52:05.110 --> 00:52:28.690
<v Ben Loory>And Death saw the fruit fall. And Death came closer until he stood at the very foot of the tree. And he turned his head and looked up through the branches and the leaves until he saw the man and the man saw him. And Death smiled and reached down and picked up the fallen fruit. And he opened his mouth and took a bite.
<v Ben Loory>死神看到了果实落下。死神走近，直到他站在树的根部。他转过头，透过树枝和树叶向上望去，直到他看到了男人，男人也看到了他。死神微笑着伸手捡起了掉落的果实。他张开嘴，咬了一口。

00:52:28.690 --> 00:52:38.390
<v Ben Loory>"Yes," said Death, in that voice that Death has. "The unripe fruits are best, I always say."
<v Ben Loory>“是的，”死神用那种死神特有的声音说。“未成熟的果实最好，我总是这么说。”

00:52:38.390 --> 00:52:46.660
<v Ben Loory>Panic seized the man. The end, he knew, was near. When Death finished the fruit, he would be next.
<v Ben Loory>恐慌抓住了男人。他知道，末日近了。当死神吃完果实，他就是下一个。

00:52:46.660 --> 00:53:12.200
<v Ben Loory>Think, thought the man. Think, think, think. But he couldn't think of a single thing to do. And then from somewhere on the other side of panic, an idea suddenly came to the man. It might work, he thought. It really just might work. And purposely, he began to shake the tree.
<v Ben Loory>思考，男人想。思考，思考，思考。但他想不出任何办法。然后，从恐慌的另一面，一个主意突然闪过男人的脑海。它可能有用，他想。它真的可能有用。他开始故意摇动那棵树。

00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:30.710
<v Ben Loory>At first, his efforts had no effect. But the man did not give up. He shook the tree with all the strength he had. He shook the tree with everything, with his entire being. And the little fruits broke free from the tree, and they rained down hard on Death.
<v Ben Loory>起初，他的努力没有效果。但男人没有放弃。他用尽全力摇动这棵树。他用尽所有，用他整个生命摇动这棵树。小果实们挣脱了树，像雨点般狠狠地砸向死神。

00:53:30.710 --> 00:53:54.690
<v Ben Loory>"Damn you, Death! Die, die, die!" yelled the man as the fruit smashed against Death's head and face and shoulders. And then with a sudden snap, all the fruits broke free, and they came down like an avalanche. And they buried Death beneath them.
<v Ben Loory>“该死，死神！死吧，死吧，死吧！”当果实砸在死神的头、脸和肩膀上时，男人大喊。然后突然间，所有的果实都挣脱了，它们像雪崩一样落下来。它们把死神埋在了下面。

00:53:54.690 --> 00:54:17.780
<v Ben Loory>For a while in the aftermath, there was only silence. And then in the silence, the man thought back. And suddenly he realized that that during the rain of fruit, Death had simply laughed and laughed and laughed. The man stayed in the tree for quite some time, until he was sure it was safe.
<v Ben Loory>之后有一段时间，只有寂静。然后在寂静中，男人回想起来。突然他意识到，在果实雨中，死神只是在不停地大笑。男人在树上待了好一阵子，直到确定安全。

00:54:17.780 --> 00:54:37.630
<v Ben Loory>And then when he climbed down very, very slowly, he stood looking at the great mound of fruit. He tapped it lightly with one foot, and the fruits all rolled aside. And there, beneath, there was no Death. Nothing, nobody, not a thing.
<v Ben Loory>当他非常、非常缓慢地爬下来时，他站在那里看着那堆巨大的果实。他用一只脚轻轻地踢了踢它，果实都滚到了一边。那里，下面，没有死神。什么都没有，没有人，什么都没有。

00:54:37.630 --> 00:55:07.020
<v Ben Loory>"Well, all right," said the man. "I guess that settles that." And he turned away and hit the road again. Not long thereafter, the man came to a hill. And when he got to the top, he looked down. And there in the valley below, he saw a figure coming towards him. A man, he thought. Just a little man.
<v Ben Loory>“好吧，好吧，”男人说。“我想这就解决了。”他转身又上了路。没过多久，男人来到一座山上。当他到达山顶时，他向下望去。在下面的山谷里，他看到一个身影正向他走来。一个人，他想。只是一个小人。

00:55:07.020 --> 00:55:42.940
<v Ben Loory>And at that very moment, the little man looked up. HE saw the man up on the hill and he froze. He turned and looked around, around the valley, panic-stricken. And he saw a nearby tree and he ran. And the man on the hilltop smiled and watched as the little man started to climb. "You think you can hide," said the man, "from me?" And he walked into the valley to see.
<v Ben Loory>就在那一刻，小人抬起头。他看到了山上的男人，僵住了。他惊慌失措地转身环顾山谷。他看到附近有一棵树，跑了过去。山顶上的男人微笑着，看着小人开始爬树。“你以为你能躲开，”男人说，“我？”于是他走进山谷去看看。

00:55:42.940 --> 00:56:21.700
<v Alex Blumberg>Ben Loory is the author of Stories for the Nighttime and Some for the Day.
<v Alex Blumberg>本·卢里是《夜晚的故事和白天的一些事》（Stories for the Nighttime and Some for the Day）的作者。

